page("campfire-breakfast-griddle","Camp Breakfast on a Compact Grill: Bacon, Eggs and Potatoes","A full camp breakfast on one small charcoal fire: bacon, crispy potatoes and eggs on a Slot Grill griddle, plus breakfast tacos, pancakes and coffee tips.",IMG["pl1"],recipe2(
"<p>Breakfast is where a griddle on a small charcoal grill really earns its spot in the camp box. One modest fire under a <a href='"+GRID+"'>Slot Grill griddle (plancha)</a> cooks bacon, crisps potatoes in the bacon fat, and fries eggs, all on one flat sheet of thick carbon steel. Owners use the Compact for exactly this: one RV owner set it up first thing to roast marshmallows for a \"sweet breakfast,\" and others recommend adding the griddle for the extra capability.</p><blockquote>\"I used it on a recent camping trip, and the food was excellent. I would recommend adding the griddle for additional capability.\"<cite>Verified Compact owner</cite></blockquote>",
"2","About 35 minutes",
["6 slices bacon","1 large potato, diced and par-cooked at home (boiled or microwaved until just tender), or leftover potatoes","½ onion and ½ bell pepper, diced (optional)","4 eggs","Salt, pepper, hot sauce","4 small tortillas and shredded cheese (optional, for tacos)","Butter or oil"],
["<b>Light a modest fire.</b> A half chimney is usually plenty. Spread coals mostly under the griddle, with a thinner area at one end for gentle cooking.","<b>Preheat the griddle</b> until a drop of water sizzles.","<b>Cook the bacon</b> on the hotter area, turning until crisp, 6–10 minutes. Move it to the cooler end or a plate.","<b>Crisp the potatoes</b> in the bacon fat on the hot side with onions and peppers. Spread in one layer and leave them to brown before turning, 8–10 minutes.","<b>Push the potatoes</b> toward the cooler end to hold.","<b>Fry the eggs</b> on a medium area with a little butter. For firm yolks, flip or cover with a small pan lid for a minute. Cook eggs until whites and yolks are firm for the safest result.","<b>Warm tortillas</b> on the grate or the griddle edge for tacos.","<b>Serve</b> with hot sauce. Scrape the griddle while warm, then oil it lightly once cool."],
why="<ul><li>The griddle gives a large flat surface for small foods that would fall through grates.</li><li>Carbon steel holds heat and evens it out across the plate.</li><li>You can put more coals under one end for searing bacon and potatoes and fewer under the other for eggs.</li></ul>",
variations=["<b>Breakfast tacos:</b> scramble eggs with the potatoes, bacon and cheese; serve in warm tortillas.","<b>Pancakes:</b> premix dry ingredients at home in a bag; add milk and egg at camp. Cook on a medium, well-oiled griddle.","<b>Sausage patties</b> instead of bacon; cook to 160°F.","<b>Steak and eggs:</b> leftover <a href='/reverse-sear-steak-small-grill.html'>ribeye</a> sliced and warmed on the griddle.","<b>French toast:</b> thick bread, egg, milk and cinnamon, cooked on a buttered medium griddle."],
trouble=[("Potatoes stick and steam","Griddle not hot or potatoes crowded. Spread out; leave them alone to brown."),("Eggs stick","Griddle needs more seasoning or fat; use medium heat."),("Bacon burns","Too much heat; move to the cooler end.")],
serve="<p>Coffee: a small kettle or percolator can sit on the grate over the hot side while you cook. Dispose of bacon grease in a heat-safe can once cooled, never on the coals or the ground at camp.</p>"),"recipes",
[("Can you cook breakfast on a charcoal grill?","Yes. A griddle plate over a modest charcoal fire cooks bacon, potatoes, eggs and pancakes. Slot Grill makes a griddle (plancha) designed for its grills."),("How much charcoal do I need for breakfast?","Usually about a half chimney on the Slot Grill Compact, spread mostly under the griddle."),("How do I keep eggs from sticking on a griddle?","Keep the griddle seasoned, use a little butter or oil, and cook eggs over medium heat rather than the hottest coals."),("Do I need to par-cook potatoes?","It helps. Par-cooking at home means they only need to crisp on the griddle at camp.")])

page("steak-fajitas-compact-grill","Steak Fajitas on a Compact Charcoal Grill","Skirt steak on the grate, peppers and onions on the griddle, one small fire. Marinade, slicing across the grain, toppings and chicken or shrimp variations.",IMG["both16"],recipe2(
"<p>Fajitas are a small-grill dream. Skirt steak is thin, so it cooks in a few minutes over hot coals, and the Compact's two-piece top lets you run a grate for the steak beside a <a href='"+GRID+"'>griddle</a> for the peppers and onions. One fire, one grill, dinner for three. Fajitas also stretch one steak across several people, which makes them a great pick when <a href='/cooking-for-a-crowd-small-grill.html'>cooking for a crowd</a>.</p>",
"3","About 40 minutes plus marinating",
["1–1½ lb skirt steak (outside skirt if available), or flank","Marinade: juice of 2 limes, 3 tablespoons oil, 3 cloves garlic (minced), 1 teaspoon cumin, 1 teaspoon chili powder, 1 teaspoon salt, ½ teaspoon black pepper","2 bell peppers and 1 large onion, sliced into strips","Oil and salt for vegetables","8–10 flour or corn tortillas","Toppings: salsa, avocado or guacamole, sour cream, cheese, cilantro, lime wedges"],
["<b>Marinate.</b> Combine marinade ingredients, add steak, and refrigerate 30 minutes to 4 hours. Citrus can make texture mushy past several hours.","<b>Light a full chimney.</b> You want high heat under the grate side and medium heat under the griddle.","<b>Start the vegetables</b> on the preheated, oiled griddle with a pinch of salt. Toss occasionally until charred at the edges and tender, 10–15 minutes. Move them to the cooler part to hold.","<b>Pat the steak dry</b> and lay it on the hot grate.","<b>Cook 2–4 minutes per side</b> for medium-rare to medium, depending on thickness. Skirt is best not overcooked. USDA's minimum for beef steaks is 145°F with a 3-minute rest.","<b>Rest</b> 5 minutes.","<b>Warm tortillas</b> on the grate, about 15–30 seconds per side.","<b>Slice thinly across the grain.</b> Skirt's grain runs across the width of the strip; cut the strip into shorter sections first, then slice each across the grain.","<b>Serve</b> steak with vegetables, tortillas and toppings."],
why="<ul><li>Thin steak + close coals = fast, deeply browned meat.</li><li>Grate and griddle side by side: steak and vegetables cook at once.</li><li>Easy to pack for camp: marinate at home in a bag.</li></ul>",
variations=["<b>Chicken fajitas:</b> boneless thighs in the same marinade; cook to 165°F.","<b>Shrimp fajitas:</b> 2–3 minutes per side; see <a href='/grilled-shrimp-compact-grill.html'>shrimp</a>.","<b>Veggie fajitas:</b> add portobello and zucchini strips.","<b>Fajita bowls:</b> serve over rice and beans."],
trouble=[("Chewy steak","Sliced with the grain or overcooked. Slice across the grain; cook hot and fast."),("Vegetables steaming","Crowded or griddle not hot. Cook in one layer; more heat."),("Marinade burns","Pat steak dry before grilling.")],
serve="<p>Leftover fajita meat makes great breakfast tacos on the griddle the next morning. Refrigerate within 2 hours.</p>"),"recipes",
[("What's the best cut for fajitas?","Skirt steak is traditional; flank also works. Both are thin, flavorful and best cooked hot and fast and sliced across the grain."),("How long do I grill skirt steak?","About 2 to 4 minutes per side over hot coals, depending on thickness."),("Can I cook fajita vegetables on the grill?","Yes. A griddle plate on the Slot Grill Compact keeps strips from falling through the grate."),("How long should I marinate fajita steak?","30 minutes to about 4 hours. Longer in a citrus marinade can affect texture.")])

page("grilled-shrimp-compact-grill","Grilled Shrimp on a Small Charcoal Grill","Garlic shrimp on skewers or the griddle over charcoal: buying, prepping, 2–3 minute cooking, and shrimp tacos. Sized for the Slot Grill Compact.",IMG["pl2"],recipe2(
"<p>Shrimp might be the fastest thing you'll ever cook over charcoal: a couple of minutes per side and they're done. That's great on a small grill because they don't need a long fire, and a single half chimney feeds two or three people. The keys are big shrimp, dry surfaces, high heat, and pulling them the moment they turn opaque.</p>",
"2–3","About 20 minutes plus 15–30 minutes marinating",
["1 lb large shrimp (16–20 or 21–25 per lb), peeled and deveined, tails on or off","3 tablespoons olive oil","3 cloves garlic, minced","1 teaspoon paprika (or smoked paprika)","½ teaspoon salt, ¼ teaspoon pepper, pinch of chili flakes","1 lemon","Chopped parsley","Skewers (soak wooden ones 30 minutes), or use the griddle"],
["<b>Thaw</b> frozen shrimp in the fridge overnight or in a sealed bag under cold water. Pat completely dry.","<b>Marinate</b> with oil, garlic, paprika, salt, pepper and chili for 15–30 minutes. Not longer with lemon; acid turns texture mushy. Add lemon after cooking.","<b>Skewer</b> through two points (head and tail end) so they don't spin, or plan to use the griddle.","<b>Light a half chimney</b> and spread coals evenly under the cooking area for a hot, even fire.","<b>Preheat and oil</b> the grate or <a href='"+GRID+"'>griddle</a>.","<b>Cook 2–3 minutes per side</b> until pink and opaque throughout. Shrimp are done when they curl into a loose C; a tight O means overcooked. Fish and shellfish should reach 145°F or be opaque throughout.","<b>Finish</b> with a squeeze of lemon and parsley."],
why="<ul><li>A small, hot fire is all shrimp need.</li><li>The griddle stops small shrimp from falling through the grates.</li><li>Fast cook = less charcoal, great for quick camp dinners.</li></ul>",
variations=["<b>Shrimp tacos:</b> chili-lime seasoning, warm tortillas, slaw and crema.","<b>Cajun-style:</b> your favorite Cajun seasoning blend with butter.","<b>Surf and turf:</b> with a <a href='/reverse-sear-steak-small-grill.html'>ribeye</a>: steak first, shrimp while it rests.","<b>Shrimp and sausage skewers</b> with peppers and onions."],
trouble=[("Rubbery","Overcooked. Pull at opaque, loose C shape."),("Sticking","Shrimp wet or grate cold. Dry, oil, preheat."),("Falling through","Use two-point skewers or the griddle.")],
serve="<p>Serve over rice, in tacos, or with grilled bread. Keep raw shrimp on ice right up until cooking, and eat within 2 hours or refrigerate.</p>"),"recipes",
[("How long do shrimp take on a charcoal grill?","About 2 to 3 minutes per side over a hot fire for large shrimp, until opaque throughout."),("How do I know shrimp are done?","They turn pink and opaque and curl into a loose C shape. Fish and shellfish should reach 145°F or be opaque throughout."),("Should I use skewers or a griddle for shrimp?","Either. Skewer through two points so they don't spin, or cook them on a griddle plate so they can't fall through."),("Can I marinate shrimp in lemon juice?","Only briefly. Acid changes texture quickly. Add lemon after cooking.")])
